olumn, and prediction markets price a Democratic win at roughly 96 to 97 percent.\n\nThe only available poll, from November 2025, showed Pritzker ahead 54 to 34 percent, though it predates the general-election campaign. Bailey has leaned on criticism of the SAFE-T Act and holds a September endorsement from President Trump, which may energize Republican turnout but did not prevent his earlier statewide loss. Recent coverage has centered as much on Pritzker's national profile and stadium politics as on a genuinely competitive race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.98, "margin": 13.88}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.983, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9789, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9753, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9721, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9755, "margin": 13.9}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9748, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9823, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9825, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9825, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9786, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9788, "margin": 14.0}]}